paths active-designing
what My own elder lap priced the extension: rye/lib/std/crypto/sha2.zig gates its SHA-NI block on a COMPTIME test, builtin.cpu.hasAll(.x86, &.{ .sha, .avx2 }), so a portable -mcpu=baseline rye pays 10.2x at Debug and 2.3x at ReleaseFast on a cold library hash. That left the card an either-or ask: portable and slow, or fast and host-bound. Press that boundary on metal rather than accept it. Measure whether Zig 0.16 can put BOTH code paths in one baseline binary and choose at startup: whether the integrated assembler admits sha256rnds2 at baseline, whether a raw-byte encoding passes where a mnemonic refuses, whether two objects built at different -mcpu link and dispatch, and what one cpuid-style detection costs against the per-call and per-build alternatives. A research paper in active-designing with the falsifier stated before the numbers; no shared file touched, scratch builds under .lap/. Aimed at Bakery's build spine and at the energy arm -- ten times the CPU work for one hash is ten times the joules.
